::: eye | How to practise with this volume
Work one topic at a time, in three passes:

1. **Read the one-liners** aloud, twice. Say each bold word before you read it.
2. **Take the past MCQs as a test.** Cover the answer line with a strip of paper, choose an option, then slide the strip down. Mark every question you got wrong.
3. **Take the practice MCQs** the same way, against the clock: about **ninety seconds** per question. Go back to the one-liner behind every wrong answer.

A week before the exam, take only the questions you marked. That list is your personal MCQ paper.
:::

# The Objective Paper

## How Part I is set

Part I has **20 MCQs**, one mark each, for **20 marks**. It must be finished in at most **30 minutes** and is answered on a separate sheet that is collected before Part II. Each question has four options, (A) to (D). Answer every question: a blank earns nothing, and a reasoned guess between two options is right half the time.

The MCQ sheets for seven years have been recovered: 2016, 2018, 2019 and 2023 to 2026, 140 questions in all. Each has been tagged with the topic it tests.

@py mcq_table

@py mcq_weights

## What the record shows

1. **Topics 1 and 2 dominate.** The discipline and social construction of gender (T1, 28 questions) and feminist theory (T2, 26) together supply more than a third of all MCQs.
2. **The paper has moved from Pakistan facts to theory.** The 2016–2019 papers were full of dates (the MFLO 1961, the harassment law 2010, CEDAW 1996). The 2023–2026 papers ask about concepts: performativity, standpoint, strategic essentialism, kyriarchy, the glass cliff.
3. **Some questions repeat almost word for word.** *The Second Sex* and its author were asked four times, the workplace harassment law five times, CEDAW's accession three times and hegemonic masculinity three times. These are free marks. Every repeat is in the past MCQs below.
4. **"None of these" is now often the answer.** In 2026, six of the twenty keys were "None of these". The examiner leaves out the right name (Patricia Hill Collins, Deniz Kandiyoti, Raja Ram Mohan Roy) to see whether you know it.
5. **Some items are defective.** A few questions have no fully correct option, or a doubtful published key (2019 Q20; 2025 Q3 and Q14). Choose the best option and move on.

::: fig flow | Attacking an MCQ in ninety seconds.
@per 5
Read the stem: underline the key word — who, when, which is NOT
Answer in your head first: before you look at the options
Match: find your answer among the options
Eliminate: strike out the options you know are wrong
Decide: "None of these" only when you know the true answer is missing
:::

::: trap | The five traps of the FPSC MCQ
1. **"Which is NOT…" and "Which is incorrect…".** Underline the NOT. Six of the twenty 2023 questions were of this kind.
2. **Near-miss dates.** 2009, 2010 and 2011 sit side by side for the harassment law. Learn the anchor dates exactly.
3. **Pairs of thinkers.** West and Zimmerman, not Parsons and Bales. Stanton and Mott, not Stanton and Anthony.
4. **"None of these".** It is the key only when the true answer is missing. Do not pick it just because you are unsure.
5. **"Both A and B" and "All of these".** Check every part. Each option must be true on its own.
:::

::: remember | The anchor dates that recur across the papers
**1949** *The Second Sex*; APWA · **1961** MFLO · **1979** CEDAW adopted; Hudood Ordinances · **1987** Connell, hegemonic masculinity · **1989** Crenshaw, intersectionality · **1990** Butler, *Gender Trouble* · **1995** Beijing · **1996** Pakistan accedes to CEDAW · **2000** NCSW; Devolution Plan (33%) · **2002** LFO (60 seats, 17%); women's policy · **2006** Women's Protection Act · **2010** workplace harassment law · **2016** honour-killing law
:::

# Topic 1 · Gender Studies and the Social Construction of Gender

> Syllabus heads I and II. 123 one-liners, 28 past MCQs and 32 practice MCQs.

The topic the objective paper visits most. The past MCQs test definitions (sex, gender, gender role, androgyny, patriarchy), the masculinity and performativity thinkers (Connell, Butler, West and Zimmerman), and Pakistan's third-gender rulings. The practice set covers what has not yet been asked: the founders of the sex/gender split (Money, Stoller, Oakley), the history of Women's Studies, language and gender, and queer theory.

